  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) Trough of low pressure will drop se into ne OH this\nafternoon and early evening. Underneath the trough, pressure gradient\nwill tighten slightly over nrn CWA, bringing a few gusts to 25 mph in\nsw flow. Southern CWA will see wind speeds of 10-15 mph without any\nsignificant gusts. Temperatures will struggle to reach the upper 50s\nwith these winds, and after morning lows near or just below freezing.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) Ridging at the surface and aloft will permit highs to\nincrease a little on Monday with upper 50s to near 60, and jump back\nup into the low to mid 60s Tuesday, and within a few degrees of 60\nWednesday. Readings drop to around 50 Thurs and bottom out in the\nmid 40s Friday, returning to near 50 on Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3) A digging system will result in the passage of a \nsurface cold front late Wednesday into Thursday. This system leads to\na mid level low that develops over the Great Lakes late in the week.\nThis will lead to a chance of rain beginning Wednesday night and \ncontinuing into Thursday. The mid level low will keep the threat for \nprecipitation in the forecast into Friday. Temperatures will turn \ncolder with highs in the 50s Thursday, the 40s for Friday, and the \nupper 40s to around 50 Saturday.",
